[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the reported outage. Data-plane and control-plane are healthy end to end from the campus host to the data-center subnet. From host_1_1_1_1, ICMP to multiple servers in 10.200.0.0/24 succeeds with 0% loss (e.g., 10.200.0.3: 3/3 received, avg RTT ~1.8 ms) and HTTP tests return promptly (curl totals ~2.5–3.0 ms to 10.200.0.3/.4/.5/.6). DNS server 10.200.0.2 is also reachable (3/3, avg ~1.5 ms).

The OSPF backbone is fully converged across the core and distribution. Each FRR device shows OSPF neighbors in Full state on the relevant interlinks: router_core_1 peers Full with router_core_2, router_core_3, and switch_dist_1_1; router_core_2 peers Full with router_core_1, router_core_3, and switch_dist_2_1; router_core_3 peers Full with router_core_1, router_core_2, and switch_server_access. No dead or init states are present, indicating stable adjacency and LSDBs.

Mechanistically, if router_core_3 or the backbone had failed, we would see loss to 10.200.0.0/24 or OSPF neighbors down; neither is observed. Localization: entire path host_1_1_1_1 → access → dist → core → server-access is operational.

Root cause: No current fault detected; the earlier symptom was likely transient during the maintenance window and resolved after OSPF reconvergence.

Corrective fix: None required; do not restart any device or link. Recommend checking FRR/OSPF logs for historical flaps and asking the user to retry; if the issue recurs, capture traceroute and timestamps for correlation. 
=== END ===